          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "There is guidance indicating that while short-acting relievers can provide quick relief of asthma symptoms, relying solely on them is not generally recommended for long-term asthma management. Clinical practice guidelines suggest that individuals with asthma typically require a combination of treatments, including inhaled corticosteroids for better control.\n\nSpecifically, the Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) emphasizes that using short-acting relievers alone can lead to poor asthma control and increased risk of severe asthma attacks. It is important for individuals with asthma to discuss their treatment plan with a healthcare provider to ensure that they are on an appropriate regimen.\n\nI could not cover specific studies or differing opinions among professionals regarding short-acting relievers, as that information is not available in the corpus.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
